# text = Doriensibus, nationi agresti et durae, montibus colendis, venationi belloque gerendo assuetae, ita quasi ad normam descripsit genus vitae, ut eorum studia antiqua ad bonam frugem dirigeret, ingenium eorum constans et firmum confirmaret, parsimoniam priscae aetatis conservaret, et ferociam insitam institutis tam sacris quam profanis temperaret, atque ab intestinis discordiis, quibus ante vexabantur, in hostes potissimum converteret.
# reading_translation = For the Dorians, a rustic and hardy nation, accustomed to cultivating mountains, hunting, and waging war, he so described their way of life, as it were, to a rule, that he directed their ancient pursuits to good fruit, confirmed their constant and firm character, preserved the frugality of the old age, tempered their innate ferocity with institutions both sacred and profane, and turned them especially from internal discords, by which they were previously troubled, against their enemies.
# literal_translation = To-the-Dorians, to-a-nation rustic and hardy, to-mountains cultivating, to-hunting and-to-war waging accustomed, thus as-if to a-rule he-described the-kind of-life, so-that their pursuits ancient to good fruit he-might-direct, the-character their constant and firm he-might-confirm, the-frugality of-old-age he-might-preserve, and the-ferocity inborn by-institutions both sacred and profane he-might-temper, and from internal discords, by-which before they-were-troubled, against enemies especially he-might-turn.
