# sent_id = orat1.1.210
# text = Cuius rei documentum publicum fertur decretum, quo animadverterint in Timotheum Milesium, artificem aetatis suae clarissimum, qui, quum Spartam venisset, et spretis antiquae Musae septem chordis undecim sonis canens, pro simplici harmonia varios et effeminatos chromatis modos induxisset, iuvenumque, ut est in decreto, corrupisset aures, atque idem Cereris Eleusiniae ludis fabulam parum decore adornans Semelae dolores docuisset pueros:
# reading_translation = A public document of this matter is reported to be a decree, by which they punished Timotheus of Miletus, the most famous artist of his age, who, when he had come to Sparta, and having despised the seven strings of the ancient Muse, singing with eleven sounds, and having introduced various and effeminate modes of the chromatic scale instead of the simple harmony, and, as it is in the decree, had corrupted the ears of the young men, and also, in the games of Ceres Eleusinia, having adorned a play with too little decorum, had taught the boys the sorrows of Semele:
# literal_translation = Of which thing a public document is reported a decree, by which they punished in Timotheus the Milesian, an artist of his age most famous, who, when he had come to Sparta, and having despised the ancient Muse's seven strings with eleven sounds singing, for simple harmony various and effeminate chromatic modes had introduced, and of the young men, as it is in the decree, had corrupted the ears, and the same in the games of Ceres Eleusinia a play too little decorously adorning the sorrows of Semele had taught the boys:
