# sent_id = orat1.2.6
# text = Etenim Ionica gens, quae, quum littora Peloponnesi septentrionalia olim habitasset, a cognatis Atticae orae incolis recepta Asiae minoris loca maritima insulasque plurimas posthac occupavit, ut ipso sedum delectu hoc, navium appulsui et mercaturae opportunissimarum, miram illam animorum agilitatem, ingenium mobile magis quam firmum, vitaeque licentioris amorem maritimis negotiis Aristotele iudice fere coniunctum declaravit:
# reading_translation = For the Ionian race, which, after having once inhabited the northern shores of the Peloponnese, was received by the kindred inhabitants of the Attic coast and later occupied the maritime regions of Asia Minor and very many islands, declared by the very choice of its abodes, most suitable for the landing of ships and for commerce, that wonderful agility of mind, a disposition more mobile than firm, and a love of a freer life, which, according to Aristotle's judgment, is generally connected with maritime pursuits:
# literal_translation = For the Ionian race, which, when it had once inhabited the northern shores of the Peloponnese, having been received by the kindred inhabitants of the Attic coast, occupied the maritime places of Asia Minor and very many islands afterwards, so that by the very choice of abodes, most suitable for the landing of ships and for commerce, it declared that wonderful agility of minds, a disposition more mobile than firm, and the love of a freer life, according to Aristotle as judge, generally connected with maritime pursuits:
