# sent_id = orat1.2.9
# text = Solonis tempore post multas factionum clades eo devenisse Athenarum res nostis, ut excusso aere alieno et nexu, quo pauperes obstringebantur nobilibus, sublato molesto illo populorum Orientis instituto, quod singulis tribubus loco, moribus, connubio segregatis suum proprium genus vitae descripserat, postremo abolita Eupatridarum potestate, et cum omnibus civibus, praeter proletarios, communicata, aequo fere iure gauderent universi, nec tamen effrenatum esset plebis imperium, quod ne quando exsisteret, tum Areopagi, sanctissimi senatus et iudicii, auctoritate summa, tum legibus caverat saluberrimis, nec tamen iis eo consilio latis, ne mutari unquam possent, sed copia data populo novarum ferendarum.
# reading_translation = You know that in the time of Solon, after many disasters of factions, the affairs of Athens had come to this point: that, after debts and the bond by which the poor were bound to the nobles had been shaken off, after that troublesome institution of the peoples of the East had been removed—which had assigned to each tribe its own proper way of life, separated by place, customs, and intermarriage—after the power of the Eupatridae had finally been abolished and shared with all citizens except the proletarians, all enjoyed nearly equal rights, and yet the rule of the common people was not unbridled; for Solon had provided, both by the supreme authority of the Areopagus, that most sacred senate and court, and by the most salutary laws, that such a thing should never arise—laws not passed with the intention that they could never be changed, but with the power given to the people to propose new ones.
# literal_translation = In-the-time-of-Solon after many of-factions disasters to-this to-have-come of-Athens the-affairs you-know, that having-been-shaken-off the-debt and the-bond, by-which the-poor were-bound to-the-nobles, having-been-removed that-troublesome of-the-peoples of-the-East institution, which to-each tribe by-place, by-customs, by-intermarriage having-been-separated its own proper kind of-life had-prescribed, finally having-been-abolished of-the-Eupatridae the-power, and with all citizens, except the-proletarians, having-been-shared, by-equal almost right they-rejoiced all, and-yet not had-been-unbridled of-the-common-people the-rule, which lest ever should-arise, then of-the-Areopagus, of-the-most-sacred senate and court, by-authority highest, then by-laws he-had-provided most-salutary, and-yet not those with-this intention having-been-passed, lest to-be-changed ever they-could, but power having-been-given to-the-people of-new to-be-proposed.
# generator = deepseek-v4-pro
