# sent_id = orat1.2.47
# text = putatis, siquid Athenis accidisset istiusmodi, ut nobilissima tragoediae poesis Solonis lege esset exsulare iussa, tantum eam emersuram temporibus proximis fuisse, ut ternos illos divini spiritus homines ad artem eam exercendam excitaret, ut Aeschyli sublimitate et magnificentia stabilita, Sophoclis perfectione absoluta, Euripidis sapientia illustrata esset, tantumque posset ad honestatem et sensum pulchri rectique formandum?
# reading_translation = Do you think that, if anything of this sort had happened in Athens, so that the noblest poetry of tragedy had been ordered into exile by Solon's law, it would have emerged so greatly in the following times that it would have roused those three men of divine spirit to practice that art, so that it was established by Aeschylus' sublimity and magnificence, perfected by Sophocles' perfection, and illustrated by Euripides' wisdom, and that it could do so much for forming honesty and the sense of the beautiful and right?
# literal_translation = Do-you-think, if anything in-Athens had-happened of-this-sort, that the-noblest poetry of-tragedy by-Solon's law had-been-ordered to-go-into-exile, so-much it would-have-emerged in-times-following, that it-would-have-roused those three men of-divine-spirit to-practice that art, that by-Aeschylus'-sublimity and-magnificence it-was-established, by-Sophocles'-perfection it-was-perfected, by-Euripides'-wisdom it-was-illustrated, and so-much it-was-able to-form honesty and sense of-beautiful and-right?
