# sent_id = orat2.2.15
# text = Atque equidem, quamquam multi dissentiunt, haud vereor affirmare, inter maxima beneficia, quibus inde a Christianae fidei incunabulis Deus mortales cumulavit, illud haud minimum esse, quod quum olim gentes eruditione eleganti conspicuae popularem civitatis statum instituissent pleraeque, regnum autem vel potius tyrannis rudes maxime et barbariae robigine obsitas nationes teneret, paullatim cultissimum quemque populum ad priscam regiae dominationis simplicitatem revocavit, quam utpote patris familias potestati proximam, constat primitivam fuisse, mox regum partim superbia, partim impotentia optimatium, partim plebis effrenatione eversam.
# reading_translation = And indeed, although many disagree, I do not hesitate to affirm that among the greatest benefits with which God has showered mortals from the very beginnings of the Christian faith, this is by no means the least: that whereas formerly the peoples distinguished by refined learning had for the most part established a popular form of government, while monarchy, or rather tyranny, held in its grip nations that were very rude and covered with the rust of barbarism, gradually He has recalled every most civilized people to the primitive simplicity of royal dominion, which, as being nearest to the authority of a father of a family, is known to have been the original form, and was afterwards overthrown partly by the pride of kings, partly by the impotence of the nobles, partly by the unbridled license of the common people.
# literal_translation = And indeed, although many disagree, not I fear to affirm, among the greatest benefits with which from the beginnings of the Christian faith God mortals has showered, that not the least is, that when formerly the peoples distinguished by elegant learning had instituted a popular state for the most part, but monarchy or rather tyranny held nations very rude and with the rust of barbarism covered, gradually every most civilized people to the primitive simplicity of royal dominion He has recalled, which as being nearest to the authority of a father of a family, it is agreed to have been primitive, and soon by the pride of kings, partly by the impotence of the nobles, partly by the unbridled license of the people overthrown.
