# sent_id = orat2.3.60
# text = at facultate data ii, qui litteris operam dabant, huius inprimis universitatis iuvenes, quin etiam doctores aliquot, quos nobis redditos fausta fortuna gratulamur, spreto, qui hanc urbem tenebat, Gallico milite inter primos arma ceperunt, suoque exemplo commonstrarunt, quanto libertatis patriaeque amore animi flagrarent artibus bonis imbuti, et quanta esset Germanorum, Borussorumque maxime virtus, qui ad disciplinas colendas natura propensi et ingeniorum luminibus clarissimis insignes, ad proelia vitaeque pericula haud essent minus parati, similes prorsus admirandis illis ad Marathonem Salaminemque proeliatoribus, qui quum sub Persico tyranno eruditioni, qua omnibus mortalibus praestabant, in otio segni vacare possent, morti incertae malebant obviam ire, quam certam pati servitutem.
# reading_translation = But when the opportunity was given, those who were devoting themselves to literature, especially the young men of this university, and even some professors, whom we congratulate ourselves on having restored to us by a happy fortune, spurned the French soldiery that held this city and were among the first to take up arms, and by their example they showed how greatly the minds imbued with the liberal arts burned with love of liberty and fatherland, and how great was the valor of the Germans, and especially of the Prussians, who, by nature inclined to the cultivation of learning and distinguished by the most brilliant lights of intellect, were no less prepared for battles and the perils of life, altogether like those admirable warriors at Marathon and Salamis, who, when under the Persian tyrant they could have devoted themselves to learning, in which they surpassed all mortals, in idle leisure, preferred to go to meet an uncertain death rather than endure certain slavery.
# literal_translation = but opportunity having-been-given those, who to literature were-giving work, of-this especially university young-men, indeed even professors some, whom to-us restored happy fortune we-congratulate, having-spurned, who this city held, French soldiery among first arms took, and by-their example showed, with-how-great of-liberty and-of-fatherland love minds burned with-arts good imbued, and how-great was of-Germans, and-of-Prussians especially valor, who to disciplines cultivating by-nature inclined and of-talents with-lights most-brilliant distinguished, for battles and-of-life perils not-at-all were less prepared, similar altogether to-admirable those at Marathon and-Salamis warriors, who when under Persian tyrant to-learning, in-which all mortals they-surpassed, in leisure idle to-be-free could, death uncertain preferred to-go-to-meet, than certain to-suffer slavery.
