# sent_id = orat3.1.7
# text = unde fit, ut antequam ad dicendum accedam, magnitudine rei spectata percelli soleam, mox collecto animo, quamvis regibus blandiundo nec natus nec factus sim, nihil mihi iucundius, nihil praestabilius videatur, quam sensus vestros, Patres, votaque pro Principe optimo suscepta pro contione tanta, tam ampla, tam splendida, tam erudita palam interpretari, ac de regiis meritis copiose disserere.
# reading_translation = Hence it happens that, before I come to speak, I am wont to be struck with the greatness of the matter when I behold it, and soon after, having collected my courage, although I am neither born nor made for flattering kings, nothing seems to me more pleasant, nothing more excellent, than openly to interpret your feelings, Fathers, and the vows undertaken for the best Prince, before so great, so ample, so splendid, so learned an assembly, and to discourse copiously on the royal merits.
# literal_translation = From which it happens, that before I may approach to speaking, by the greatness of the matter having been beheld I am accustomed to be struck, soon with the mind having been collected, although for flattering kings neither born nor made I may be, nothing to me more pleasant, nothing more excellent may seem, than the senses yours, Fathers, and the vows for the best Prince undertaken before an assembly so great, so ample, so splendid, so learned openly to interpret, and about the royal merits copiously to discourse.
