# sent_id = orat3.2.18
# text = quin a Caesare per praeconem evocatus, ut regni usurpati religionisque pristinae exutae rationem redderet, et creditoribus suis satisfaceret, edictoque in ipsa curia Regiomonte promulgato, tandem in comitiis Augustanis terra Borussica Gualtero Cronbergio, Magistro ordinis addicta, aut principatu decedere aut in supremo Imperii iudicio causam agere additis proscriptionis minis iussus, postremo, ut praedictum erat, proscriptus in discrimen summum adducebatur, ni fides subiectorum plus quam Caesaris ira valuisset, et fortuna Albertum adiuvisset copias imperatorias bellis gravioribus detinens.
# reading_translation = Indeed, when he was summoned by Caesar through a herald to give an account of his usurped rule and of his abandoned former religion, and to satisfy his creditors, and after an edict was published in the very senate-house at Königsberg, finally in the assemblies at Augsburg, when the Prussian land was assigned to Walter von Cronberg, Master of the Order, he was ordered either to withdraw from the principality or to plead his case in the supreme court of the Empire, with threats of proscription added; and at last, as had been foretold, being proscribed, he was brought into the utmost peril, had not the loyalty of his subjects prevailed more than Caesar's anger, and had fortune not aided Albert by keeping the imperial forces occupied with more serious wars.
# literal_translation = Indeed, by Caesar through a herald having been summoned, that of the usurped rule and of the former religion abandoned an account he should render, and to his creditors he should satisfy, and with an edict in the very senate-house at Königsberg having been published, finally in the assemblies at Augsburg, with the Prussian land to Walter Cronberg, Master of the Order, having been assigned, either from the principality to depart or in the supreme judgment of the Empire the case to plead, with threats of proscription added, having been ordered, at last, as had been foretold, having been proscribed, into the utmost danger he was being brought, unless the loyalty of subjects more than the anger of Caesar had prevailed, and fortune Albert had aided, the imperial forces with more serious wars detaining.
