# sent_id = orat4.1.7
# text = quo die ego huc vocatus, ut quod mihi ex re videretur, paucis dissererem, simul ut Regi augusto beneficii in nos collati grates agerem, quamquam mihi tempus perexiguum concessum et quasi ad aquam dicendum est, ad sollemnium dignitatem argumenti praestantia aliqua ex parte studens accedere, religioni duco de re ulla dicere quam de ipsa instituti gravissimi ratione, qui finis, quod ingenium sit doctrinae disciplinaeque academicae, quid Rex sapientissimus in schola hac artium condenda maxime spectasse videatur, denique quid nobis factum et posthac faciendum sit, quo studii litterarum indoli voluntatique regiae optime respondeamus.
# reading_translation = On the day on which I was summoned here to discuss briefly what seemed to me appropriate, and at the same time to give thanks to the august King for the benefit conferred upon us, although a very short time has been granted to me and I must speak almost as if by the water-clock, striving to approach the dignity of the solemn occasion by some excellence of argument, I consider it a matter of religious scruple to speak on any subject other than the very nature of this most weighty institution: what is the purpose, what is the character of academic learning and discipline, what the most wise King seems to have had chiefly in view in founding this school of arts, and finally what has been done by us and what must be done hereafter so that we may best respond to the character of literary study and to the royal will.
# literal_translation = on which day I hither summoned, that what to me from the matter might seem, in few words I might discuss, at the same time that to the King august for the benefit upon us conferred thanks I might give, although to me time very short granted and as if to the water it must be spoken is, to the solemn occasion's dignity by argument's excellence in some part striving to approach, a religious scruple I deem about any matter to speak than about the very of the institution most weighty nature, what the end, what the character is of learning and discipline academic, what the King most wise in school this of arts founding chiefly to have had in view seems, finally what by us done and hereafter must be done, by which to the study of letters' character and to the will royal best we may respond.
