# sent_id = orat4.5.15
# text = Tum Electori una cum lectissimis ministrorum, ex quibus Paulum Fuchsium, oratorem Principis, Ludolphum et Everardum Dankelmannos, et insigne illud litterarum decus Ezechielem Spanhemium nomino, et cum Ordinibus provinciae inter aeris campani sonitus et tormentorum Magdeburgo advectorum tonitrua musicosque concentus splendido agmine urbem ingressuro obviam fuerunt equitatus iuvenum nobilium circiter centum et quinquaginta, strictis gladiis, ut, quae Cellarii verba sunt, aeque se a Martis quam ab Apollinis disciplina paratos comprobarent, et professores cum Senatu urbano ac sacerdotibus.
# reading_translation = Then the Elector, together with the most distinguished of his ministers—among whom I name Paul Fuchs, the prince's orator, Ludolph and Everard Dankelmann, and that distinguished ornament of letters, Ezekiel Spanheim—and with the provincial estates, amid the sounds of the bronze trumpet and the thunder of cannons brought from Magdeburg and musical harmonies, was met on his entry into the city with a splendid procession by a cavalry of about one hundred and fifty noble youths, with drawn swords, so that, as Cellarius's words are, they might prove themselves equally prepared in the discipline of Mars as in that of Apollo, and by the professors with the city senate and the priests.
# literal_translation = Then to-the-Elector together with the-most-selected of-ministers, from whom Paul Fuchs, the-orator of-the-Prince, Ludolph and Everard Dankelmanns, and that distinguished ornament of-letters Ezekiel Spanheim I-name, and with the-Orders of-the-province amid the-sounds of-the-bronze-trumpet and the-thunders of-cannons to-Magdeburg brought and musical-concentuses with-splendid procession to-the-city about-to-enter were-encountered by-cavalry of-youths noble about one-hundred and fifty, with-drawn swords, so-that, which Cellarius's words are, equally themselves from Mars's than from Apollo's discipline prepared they-might-prove, and by-professors with-Senate urban and with-priests.
