# sent_id = orat5.3.1
# text = Sed apud populares nostros litterae, quae per viros in Italia excultos, et maxime per Rudolphum Agricolam olim erant stabilitae, ad fontium sacrorum intelligentiam accommodatae a Reuchlino et Erasmo sunt, quorum alter Hebraica docuit, alter non solum de libris novi foederis pro illius aetatis habitu optime meritus est, verum etiam scholasticas tricas multis pridem invisas sensim et pedetentim removit, ac stultitiam temporum sale perfricuit, ipse magnus sine dubio ut studiorum, sic rerum sacrarum reformator futurus, nisi iocari quam serio agere, et prae ambitione ac timiditate gratiam potentum aucupari suaque consilia ad aliena componere, quam simplici ac forti animo, quae sibi recta viderentur, dicere praetulisset:
# reading_translation = But among our countrymen, the scholarship which had once been established by men of culture in Italy, and especially by Rudolph Agricola, was adapted to the understanding of the sacred sources by Reuchlin and Erasmus. Of these two, the one taught Hebrew, the other not only rendered the greatest service to the books of the New Testament, considering the character of that age, but also gradually and step by step removed the scholastic trifles that had long been hated by many, and rubbed away the folly of the times with his wit. He himself would undoubtedly have been a great reformer of both learning and sacred matters, had he not preferred to jest rather than to act seriously, and, out of ambition and timidity, to court the favor of the powerful and to adapt his own plans to those of others, rather than to speak with a simple and brave spirit what seemed right to him.
# literal_translation = But among countrymen our the scholarship, which through men in Italy cultivated, and especially through Rudolph Agricola once had been established, to of the sources sacred understanding adapted by Reuchlin and Erasmus are, of whom the one Hebrew taught, the other not only concerning the books of the new covenant for of that age the character best has deserved, but also scholastic trifles to many long hated gradually and step by step removed, and the folly of the times with wit rubbed away, himself great without doubt as of studies, so of matters sacred a reformer about to be, unless to jest than seriously to act, and before ambition and timidity the favor of the powerful to hunt for and his own plans to others' to adapt, than with simple and brave spirit, what to himself right might seem, to speak he had preferred:
