# sent_id = orat6.2.1
# text = Iam vero quum hic annus rei publicae quum universae tum maxime litterariae mutatione ministrorum regiorum, qui summae rerum praepositi sunt, memorabilis fuerit, quis est quin intelligat, huic universitati Regem optime consuluisse, quod, quum ille nobis eriperetur, qui tot per annos res nostras bene, diligenter, strenue, integre curaverat, cui multa et praeclara iam debebamus, plura sine dubio debituri eramus, postremo quocum diuturno usu quandam quasi familiaritatem contraxeramus, quique in ipso abdicationis momento, quam ipsi cari essemus, candide declaravit, vir nobis praefectus est, quo neque digniorem consensus hominum monstrare, nec peritiorem eruditio comprobare, neque promptiorem, liberaliorem, humaniorem experientia edocti desiderare et expetere queant:
# reading_translation = Now, since this year has been memorable for the state, both in general and especially in literary affairs, because of the change of royal ministers who are placed in charge of the highest matters, who does not understand that the King has taken the best care of this university? For when he who had managed our affairs well, diligently, strenuously, and uprightly for so many years was taken from us—he to whom we already owed many splendid things, and to whom we would undoubtedly have owed more; with whom, through long association, we had formed a kind of intimacy; and who, at the very moment of his resignation, candidly declared how dear we were to him—a man has been set over us than whom the consensus of men could not point out one more worthy, nor learning approve one more skilled, nor could those taught by experience desire and seek one more ready, generous, or humane.
# literal_translation = Now truly since this year for the state both in general and especially in literary by the change of ministers royal, who to the highest matters are placed in charge, memorable has been, who is there who does not understand, for this university the King best to have consulted, because, when that man from us was snatched away, who through so many years our affairs well, diligently, strenuously, uprightly had managed, to whom many and splendid things already we owed, more without doubt we would have owed, finally with whom by long use a certain as it were familiarity we had contracted, and who in the very moment of resignation, how dear to him we were, candidly declared, a man to us has been set over, than whom neither more worthy the consensus of men could show, nor more skilled learning could approve, nor more ready, more generous, more humane those taught by experience could desire and seek:
