# sent_id = orat6.3.3
# text = Tum vero qui in hac regia urbe vel adolescentium aliquot licentiam, quae facillimis remediis compesci potest, maius malum quam litteras bonum esse censebant, vel nescio quas ob causas non ferendam hanc eruditionis palaestram adhuc multis insuetam arbitrabantur, rumorem de transferenda vel delenda schola nostra studiosissime sparserunt, ne reverentia quidem, quae Principi debetur, observata, quum Regem constantissimum, dum eum de iis, quae anno ante sanxerat, rescindendis cogitare ementiuntur, manifestae inconstantiae insimularent.
# reading_translation = Then indeed those who in this royal city either considered the license of some young men, which could be restrained by the easiest remedies, a greater evil than literature was a good, or thought that this palaestra of learning, still unfamiliar to many, was not to be endured for some reasons or other, most eagerly spread the rumor about transferring or abolishing our school, not even observing the reverence which is owed to the Prince, when they falsely accused the most steadfast King of manifest inconstancy, while they pretended that he was thinking about rescinding what he had decreed the year before.
# literal_translation = Then indeed who in this royal city either the license of some young men, which by the easiest remedies can be restrained, a greater evil than literature a good to be thought, or I-know-not-what on account of causes not to be endured this palaestra of learning still to many unaccustomed judged, a rumor about transferring or abolishing our school most eagerly spread, not even reverence, which to the Prince is owed, having been observed, when the King most steadfast, while him about those things, which in the year before he had sanctioned, about rescinding to think they falsely pretend, of manifest inconstancy they accused.
