# sent_id = orat6.5.17
# text = Igitur quum hoc ii non ignorent, qui rem publicam ista ratione administrari, quam pro re nata prudenter emendari malunt, cordatos eruditionis et sapientiae magistros tanquam corruptores ingeniorum et civitatis incusant, qui quum rerum gerendarum imperiti ipsi sint, tamen pragmaticorum rationem reprehendere superciliosa censura audeant, iuvenesque a recta scilicet consuetaque negotiorum via subtilitate disputandi inanibusque declamationibus avertere moliantur.
# reading_translation = Therefore, since those who prefer that the state be administered in that manner rather than wisely corrected according to circumstances do not ignore this, they accuse the learned and wise teachers, as corrupters of talents and of the state, who, although they themselves are inexperienced in conducting affairs, nevertheless dare to criticize the method of practical men with haughty censure, and strive to turn young men away from the right and customary path of business by the subtlety of argument and empty declamations.
# literal_translation = Therefore since this those not ignore, who the state in that manner to be administered, than according to circumstances wisely to be corrected prefer, learned and wise teachers as corrupters of talents and of the state they accuse, who since of things to be done inexperienced themselves are, nevertheless the method of practical men to criticize with haughty censure dare, and young men from the right indeed customary path of business with subtlety of disputing and empty declamations to turn away strive.
