# text = Socrates philosophus, qui etsi a rebus gerendis alienus earumque adeo nescius, ut civibus, quos in suffragium mittere vellet, risum excitaret, doctrina et admonitione id agebat, ut rei publicae forma in meliorem frugem converteretur et secundum praecepta sapientiae constitueretur ac gubernaretur, ab Anyto eiusque asseclis, plebeculae famulis, quibus ista rerum condicio optima videbatur, impietatis accusatus et supplicio affectus est:
# reading_translation = Socrates the philosopher, although he was estranged from public affairs and indeed so ignorant of them that he would provoke laughter from the citizens whom he wanted to send to vote, yet by his teaching and admonition he strove that the form of the state might be changed for the better and be established and governed according to the precepts of wisdom; he was accused of impiety and punished by Anytus and his followers, servants of the common people, to whom that condition of affairs seemed best.
# literal_translation = Socrates the philosopher, who although from affairs to-be-done alien and of-them indeed so ignorant, that to-citizens, whom into vote to-send he-wanted, laughter he-would-excite, by-teaching and admonition this he-was-doing, that of-the-republic form into better fruit might-be-turned and according-to precepts of-wisdom might-be-established and might-be-governed, by Anytus and his followers, of-the-rabble servants, to-whom that of-affairs condition best seemed, of-impiety accused and with-punishment affected was:
