# sent_id = orat6.7.9
# text = Unde si nec civitates neque litterae detrimentum ceperunt, haec potissima causa est, quod artes bonae sua ipsarum praestantia amplissimum et clarissimum quemque, et qui apud suos plurimum valeret, ita allexerant, ut non modo doctis eximie faveret, neque ullis sumptibus in illis remunerandis parceret, sed doctissimus ipse esset, et quod ea omnino illius aetatis liberalitas erat, ut qui rebus gerendis excellere vellent, eruditionis elegantia et ubertate ceteris sese praestare debere arbitrarentur.
# reading_translation = Therefore, if neither the states nor literature suffered any loss, the most important reason is that the good arts, by their own excellence, had so attracted every most distinguished and most famous man, and whoever had the greatest influence among his own people, that he not only favored learned men exceptionally and spared no expense in rewarding them, but was himself most learned; and because the liberality of that age was such that those who wished to excel in public affairs thought that they ought to surpass others in the elegance and richness of their learning.
# literal_translation = Whence if neither the states nor the literature damage received, this the-most-important cause is, that the good arts by-their-own excellence the-most-distinguished and the-most-famous each-one, and who among his-own most was-able, so had-attracted, that not only the-learned exceptionally he-favored, nor any expenses in rewarding-them spared, but most-learned himself was, and that that entirely of-that-age liberality was, that who in-things-to-be-done to-excel wished, of-learning elegance and abundance others themselves to-surpass ought they-thought.
