# sent_id = orat7.5.39
# text = Rerum gestarum vero cognitio praeterquam quod ei, qui ad rem publicam accedere aut in ullo litterarum genere habitare constituerit, omnino necessaria est, humano cultui eo est coniunctior, quod generis humani vicissitudines prosperaeque et adversae fortunae causas et origines ostendit, quod summa virtutis et vitiorum exempla docet, quod rectam rerum aestimationem, quidque bonum malumve iudicandum sit, experientia duce declarat, denique quod quidquid morum philosophia praeceptis complectitur, id latissimo rerum gestarum campo aperiendo quasi ante oculos propositum repraesentat.
# reading_translation = But the knowledge of history, besides being absolutely necessary for one who has decided to enter public life or to dwell in any branch of literature, is more closely connected with human culture, because it shows the vicissitudes of the human race and the causes and origins of prosperous and adverse fortune, because it teaches the greatest examples of virtue and vice, because it declares, with experience as guide, the right estimation of things and what is to be judged good or bad, and finally because whatever the philosophy of morals embraces in its precepts, that it presents as if placed before the eyes by opening the broadest field of history.
# literal_translation = Of-things-done indeed knowledge besides-that to-him who to public-affairs to-approach or in any of-literature kind to-dwell has-decided, altogether necessary is, to-human-culture so is more-connected, because of-human-race vicissitudes and-prosperous and-adverse fortune causes and origins shows, because greatest of-virtue and-of-vices examples teaches, because right of-things estimation, and-what good or-evil to-be-judged is, with-experience leader declares, finally because whatever of-morals philosophy with-precepts embraces, that in-broadest of-things-done field by-opening as-if before eyes placed presents.
