# sent_id = orat8.1.8
# text = Itaque hoc die, qui conditoris nostri nomini sacer est, his temporibus, in hoc discrimine civitatium litterariarum, quum non solum varii generis hominum de illarum indole diversissimae sententiae proferantur, sed etiam supremum Germaniae Principum concilium universitatibus emendandis et curatius regendis mentem studiose adverterit, singulisque suos procuratores praefecerit, qui et docentes et discentes observarent, imprimisque caverent, ne quid in iis radices ageret, quod harum scholarum consilio et ingenio, quale esse debeat, adversaretur:
# reading_translation = Therefore on this day, which is sacred to the name of our founder, at this time, in this crisis of the literary states, when not only are very different opinions of men of various kinds about their nature put forward, but also the supreme council of the princes of Germany has zealously turned its mind to the improvement and more careful governance of the universities, and has set over each one its own overseers, who would observe both teachers and learners, and above all would take care that nothing should take root in them that would be contrary to the purpose and character of these schools, as it ought to be:
# literal_translation = Therefore on this day, which is sacred to the name of our founder, in these times, in this crisis of the literary states, when not only of various kinds of men about their nature very different opinions are put forward, but also the supreme council of the princes of Germany to the universities for improving and for more carefully governing the mind zealously has turned, and to each one its own overseers has set over, who both teachers and learners would observe, and especially would take care, lest anything in them roots should take, which of these schools to the purpose and character, such as it ought to be, would be contrary:
