# sent_id = orat8.3.8
# text = tamen tantum abest, ut in hoc studiorum fuerit summa posita, ut etiam multi discipulos a re publica, qualis tum fuit, attingenda revocaverint, et ii potissimum, qui studia lucri vel vanae gloriolae causa tractarent, sophistae usum rei publicae praetexuerint, denique civitates ipsae nihil ad sese illas litteras pertinere arbitratae sint, nisi quod eruditionis splendore captae philosophos, rhetores, grammaticos reveritae sunt summisque honoribus dignos ut plurimum habuerunt, et longe rarius doctrinae lumina reformidantes, sapientes doctosque viros calumniis circumventos exsilio vel morte affecerunt, aut labante rei publicae flore, quod Athenis auctore Sophocle evenit, libertatem docendi sustulerunt.
# reading_translation = Yet so far is it from being the case that the highest importance was placed on these studies, that many even called their students away from engaging in public affairs, such as they then were, and those especially who pursued studies for the sake of profit or vain little glory, the sophists, made a pretext of the practical use of public life; in short, the states themselves thought that those literary pursuits had nothing to do with them, except that, captivated by the splendor of learning, they revered philosophers, rhetoricians, and grammarians, and for the most part deemed them worthy of the highest honors; and far more rarely, fearing the lights of learning, they destroyed wise and learned men, beset by slanders, by exile or death, or, when the flower of the state was tottering, as happened at Athens on the motion of Sophocles, they took away the freedom of teaching.
# literal_translation = nevertheless so-much it-is-absent, that in this of-studies might-have-been the-highest importance placed, that also many students from public affair, of-what-sort then it-was, to-be-touched called-back, and those especially, who studies of-profit or of-vain little-glory for-the-sake-of were-handling, sophists the-use of-public affair made-a-pretext, finally states themselves nothing to themselves those letters to-pertain thought, unless that of-learning by-the-splendor captivated philosophers, rhetoricians, grammarians revered and with-the-highest honors worthy as for-the-most-part held, and far more-rarely of-learning the-lights fearing, wise and-learned men by-slanders surrounded by-exile or by-death afflicted, or tottering of-the-state the-flower, which at-Athens on-the-motion-of Sophocles happened, the-freedom of-teaching took-away.
