# text = quod quum vel ii, qui in rebus gerendis summa cum laude et sollertia versati sint, nisi litterarum insignior peritia accesserit, academicos honores ambire nullo modo queant, et contra qui negotiis publicis quamvis impares eruditione praestent, illis honoribus capessendis unice digni iudicentur, in artium et doctrinarum tractatione, non in hominibus ad negotia gerenda fingendis academicae disciplinae summam reponendam esse patet.
# reading_translation = Since even those who have been engaged in public affairs with the highest praise and skill cannot in any way aspire to academic honors unless they have acquired a more distinguished proficiency in letters, and on the other hand those who, although unequal to public business, excel in learning are judged uniquely worthy of obtaining those honors, it is clear that the essence of academic discipline is to be placed in the cultivation of arts and sciences, not in shaping men for conducting business.
