# text = Et illud quidem tantum beneficium antiquitus academiis collatum paullatim ita esse imminutum, ut praeter inania vocabula nullum prope pristinae dignitatis vestigium relictum sit, est profecto eo magis dolendum, quod non qui privilegiorum tantorum vim fregerunt, accusandi sunt, sed qui partim rem gravissimam olim visam leviter et negligenter curantes partim lucri cupidine ducti, imperitos et incultos homines ad honores academicos evexerunt.
# reading_translation = And that great benefit, indeed, formerly conferred upon the academies, has gradually been so diminished that, apart from empty names, scarcely any trace of their former dignity remains; and this is certainly the more to be lamented, because not those who broke the force of such great privileges are to be accused, but those who, partly treating a matter once regarded as most serious lightly and negligently, and partly led by desire for gain, have raised ignorant and uncultivated men to academic honors.
