# sent_id = orat8.12.2
# text = Nam ne nunc a nostris rebus discedam, quis non intelligat magnificos hos apparatus et cuiusvis generis doctrinarum subsidia amplissima et splendidissima, quae non modo huic universitati litterariae, sed ipsi regiae urbi ornamento sunt, et quae Regis benignissimi munificentia et viri illius excellentissimi, qui institutioni publicae in hoc imperio praeest, prudenti et liberali cura magis in dies augentur, haec inquam non alio consilio comparata esse, nisi ut rerum optimarum disciplina non sordida aliqua de causa, sed ob insitam praestantiam pertractetur latiusque et altius excolatur?
# reading_translation = For not to depart now from our own affairs, who would not understand that these magnificent preparations and the most ample and most splendid supports of learning of every kind, which are an ornament not only to this literary university but to the royal city itself, and which by the munificence of the most kind King and by the prudent and liberal care of that most excellent man who presides over public instruction in this empire are daily increased more and more, these, I say, have been provided with no other purpose than that the study of the best things should be pursued not for some sordid reason but on account of its inherent excellence and should be cultivated more widely and more deeply?
# literal_translation = For not now from our affairs may I depart, who not may understand that magnificent these preparations and of-every-kind of-disciplines supports most-ample and most-splendid, which not only to-this literary-university, but to-the-royal-city-itself ornament are, and which of-the-King most-kind munificence and of-the-man that most-excellent, who to-public-instruction in-this-empire presides, by-prudent and-liberal care more by-days are-increased, these I-say not with-other plan prepared to-be, unless that of-best-things discipline not for-sordid some reason, but on-account-of inherent excellence be-pursued and-more-widely and-more-deeply be-cultivated?
