# sent_id = orat9.2.15
# text = et quum alii Aegyptum, Atheniensibus iam prius perniciosam, Siciliam, quae post Periclem Atticis copiis exitio fuit, Etruriam, Carthaginem affectandam arbitrarentur, noluit cives in discrimen dare, sed dux belli in ea potissimum re gloriam quaesivit, nihil ut nimis arduum aut anceps susciperet, militesque, quod ipse dicebat, quantum penes ipsum esset, possent immortales esse.
# reading_translation = And although others thought that Egypt, which had already been ruinous to the Athenians before, Sicily, which after Pericles was destruction to the Attic forces, Etruria, and Carthage should be attempted, he was unwilling to put the citizens at risk, but sought glory as a war leader especially in this, that he would undertake nothing too difficult or uncertain, and that the soldiers, as he himself used to say, could, as far as it lay in his power, be immortal.
# literal_translation = And when others Egypt, to-the-Athenians already before ruinous, Sicily, which after Pericles to-the-Attic forces destruction was, Etruria, Carthage to-be-attempted thought, he-was-unwilling citizens into danger to-give, but leader of-war in this especially thing glory sought, nothing so-that too difficult or uncertain he-would-undertake, and-soldiers, which he-himself said, as-much-as in-himself it-were, could immortal to-be.
