# sent_id = orat9.3.11
# text = Neque huic uni placitorum generi addictus, quod in viro civitati praeposito maxime laudaveris, quem varias philosophandi rationes nosse et tolerare, non alteram prae alterius studio contemnere vel praecludere oportet, Pericles diversae plane disciplinae sapienti, Anaxagorae physico, ingentis animi viro, qui divino philosophandi impetu ductus domum agrosque reliquerat, imprimis deditus fuit eumque insigniter admiratus est.
# reading_translation = And not being devoted to this one kind of doctrine alone, which you would especially praise in a man placed at the head of a state, whom it behooves to know and tolerate various systems of philosophy, not to despise or exclude one in preference to another, Pericles was especially devoted to Anaxagoras, a wise man of a quite different school, a natural philosopher, a man of great spirit, who, led by a divine impulse of philosophizing, had left his house and fields, and he admired him remarkably.
