# sent_id = orat14.2.24
# text = idque etiam usu et auctoritate ita evincitur, ut in pluribus academiis atque etiam apud nos aliquot medicinae professores ab arte salutari factitanda procul remoti in sola rerum natura perscrutanda occupati sint, et nuperrime a viris excellentissimis et illustrissimis, quibus Fridericus Guilelmus hanc rei publicae partem mandavit, constitutum sapienter sit, ne quis ad summos in medicina honores capessendos admitteretur, nisi rerum naturalium cognitionem sufficientem peculiari tentamine in hunc finem subeundo probasset.
# reading_translation = And this is even proved by practice and authority, so that in many universities, and even among us, several professors of medicine, far removed from the practice of the healing art, are occupied solely in investigating nature, and very recently it was wisely established by the most excellent and most distinguished men, to whom Frederick William entrusted this part of the state, that no one should be admitted to seeking the highest honors in medicine unless he had proved his sufficient knowledge of natural things by undergoing a special examination for this purpose.
# literal_translation = And this even by use and authority so is proved, that in many academies and even among us several professors of medicine from the art of healing to be practiced far removed in the nature of things alone to be investigated occupied are, and very recently by men most excellent and most distinguished, to whom Frederick William this part of the republic entrusted, established wisely has been, that no one to the highest in medicine honors to be sought admitted would be, unless of natural things knowledge sufficient by a special examination for this end to be undergone had proved.
