# sent_id = orat15.3.25
# text = Neque rex in omnibus rei publicae gerendae partibus ea instructus prudentia esse necessario debet, quam ab iis poscimus, qui singulas quasque tractant partes, sed sicuti qui olim ingenii et eloquentiae viribus plebem regebant oratores nec iuris nec militiae nec rationis redituum et vectigalium magnopere periti erant, sed tamen rem publicam gubernabant non sine magna sollertia, ita regia ars universam civitatem moderatur eatenus, ut singulis quidem rebus singulos praeficiat administros, quibus quasi instrumentis utatur, ipsa vero summam omnium, quas illi praeparaverint, rerum complectatur, quae singularum partium praefecti suppeditarint, diiudicet, potissima decernat, quae agenda sint iubeat et mandet.
# reading_translation = Nor does a king necessarily have to be equipped with that prudence in all parts of administering the state which we demand from those who handle each individual part, but just as the orators who once governed the people by the power of their intellect and eloquence were not greatly skilled in law or warfare or the management of revenues and taxes, yet they steered the state not without great cleverness, so the royal art governs the whole community to the extent that it appoints individual administrators for individual matters, whom it uses as instruments, while it itself embraces the sum of all the matters which they have prepared, judges what the overseers of the individual parts have supplied, decides the most important things, and orders and entrusts what must be done.
# literal_translation = Nor a-king in all of-the-state of-governing parts with-that equipped prudence to-be necessarily ought, which from them we-demand, who individual each handle parts, but just-as who once of-intellect and of-eloquence by-powers the-people governed orators neither of-law nor of-warfare nor of-management of-revenues and of-taxes greatly skilled were, but nevertheless the-state steered not without great cleverness, so royal art the-whole community governs to-that-extent, that for-individual indeed things individual it-puts-in-charge administrators, whom as instruments it-uses, itself truly the-sum of-all, which those have-prepared, of-things embraces, which of-individual of-parts the-overseers have-supplied, judges, most-important decides, what to-be-done are orders and entrusts.
