# sent_id = orat15.3.42
# text = Sic, ut exemplo utar nobis proximo, litteras nosse aliquatenus et magni aestimare decorum etiam principi est, et litteraria certe civitatis instituta regere quodammodo ipse debet, et quae horum vis et indoles sit ac quomodo optime ordinentur, intelligere, non tamen, quemadmodum Dionysius Siciliae tyrannus cum poetis, Nero cum cantoribus, Hadrianus Imperator, Henricus Octavus et Iacobus Primus Angliae reges cum eruditis immodico certabant studio, laudem doctrinae affectare, ut scilicet in hoc quoque genere princeps habeatur.
# reading_translation = Thus, to use a very recent example, it is fitting even for a prince to have some knowledge of letters and to value them highly, and he himself ought in some measure to direct the literary institutions of the state, and to understand what their force and character are and how they may best be organized; yet he should not, as Dionysius the tyrant of Sicily strove with poets, Nero with singers, the Emperor Hadrian, Henry VIII and James I kings of England with the learned, with immoderate zeal, aspire to the praise of learning, so that of course in this kind too he may be regarded as a prince.
# literal_translation = Thus, so that I may use an example nearest to us, to know letters to some extent and to esteem greatly is becoming even for a prince, and the literary institutions of the state certainly he himself ought in some way to rule, and what the force and character of these may be and how they may be best arranged, to understand, not however, just as Dionysius the tyrant of Sicily with poets, Nero with singers, Hadrian the Emperor, Henry the Eighth and James the First kings of England with the learned contended with immoderate zeal, to aspire to the praise of learning, so that of course in this also kind he may be held a prince.
