# text = Atqui patriae caritatem et principis amorem officio satisfacientis ita consociata inter populos, qui regnantur, invenimus, ut qui patriam diligat, non possit non principem diligere, et quum principis persona universam in sese civitatem contineat velut adspectabili imagine expressam, sine gratissimo illo, quo caput rei publicae amplectimur, affectu ne patria quidem possit satis cara nostris pectoribus esse.
# reading_translation = But we find the love of country and the love of the prince, who satisfies his duty, so united among the peoples who are ruled, that he who loves his country cannot but love the prince, and since the person of the prince contains in itself the whole state as expressed in a visible image, without that most grateful affection with which we embrace the head of the republic, not even the country could be dear enough to our hearts.
# literal_translation = But the love of country and the love of the prince satisfying his duty so united among the peoples who are ruled we find, that he who loves the country, not can not love the prince, and when the person of the prince in itself the whole state contains as in a visible image expressed, without that most grateful, with which the head of the republic we embrace, affection, not even the country indeed can sufficiently dear to our hearts be.
