# sent_id = orat17.3.6
# text = sic inquam Sparta et Creta prope unice militares fuerunt, quod optimatium exercitus praepotens ceteris imperabat ut servis, agros colebant clientes dure ut plurimum habiti, atque ut agriculturae opus quamvis utile et delectabile, sic mercatura, opificia, reliquus quaestus in nullo erant honore, litterae vero et artes, exceptis gymnicis et musica et siqua alia numinis colendi causa tractanda visa, nimium negligebantur, civesque, quod eximie perspexit Plato, non ad virtutem universam, cuius partibus omnibus perfusa esse res publica debet, sed ad solam fere fortitudinem formabantur;
# reading_translation = Thus, I say, Sparta and Crete were almost uniquely military states, because the powerful army of the aristocrats ruled over the rest as if they were slaves; the clients, for the most part harshly treated, cultivated the fields; and just as the work of agriculture, however useful and delightful, so too trade, crafts, and other pursuits were held in no honor; indeed, letters and arts, except for gymnastics and music and any other things deemed necessary for the worship of the deity, were excessively neglected; and the citizens, as Plato excellently perceived, were trained not for universal virtue, with all of whose parts the state ought to be imbued, but almost solely for courage.
# literal_translation = Thus I-say Sparta and Crete almost uniquely military were, because of-the-aristocrats army very-powerful to-the-rest ruled as to-slaves, fields cultivated clients harshly for-the-most-part treated, and as of-agriculture work however useful and delightful, so trade, crafts, remaining pursuit in no were honor, letters indeed and arts, excepted gymnastic and music and if-any other of-deity worshipping for-the-sake-of to-be-handled seen, too-much were-neglected, citizens-and, what excellently perceived Plato, not to virtue universal, of-which parts all having-been-perfused the state ought, but to alone almost courage were-formed;
# generator = deepseek-v4-pro
