# sent_id = orat17.3.106
# text = Neque immerito in fine commentarioli sophisticam et minutiis adhibitam Isocratis curam spernere prae Chabria et Iphicrate videtur, quod is, dum hi victoriis Graeciam conciliant patriae, domi deses cincinnos orationis torqueret, et nonam longissimae vitae partem, ut ait Chaeronensis philosophus, hoc est prope tres Olympiades, quot annis Hecatompedum et Propylaea exstruxerit Pericles, consumeret in retractandis Panegyrici verbis, magis quam miles sonum armorum et phalangum concursum et vulnera, metuens ille ne vocalem cum vocali collideret, vel membrum, quod dicunt, aequale una syllaba proferret brevius.
# reading_translation = And not undeservedly, at the end of his little commentary, he seems to scorn the sophistical care applied to trifles by Isocrates, preferring Chabrias and Iphicrates, because while they were winning Greece for their fatherland by victories, he, idle at home, would twist the curls of his oratory, and would spend the ninth part of a very long life—as the philosopher of Chaeronea says, that is, nearly three Olympiads, the number of years in which Pericles built the Hecatompedon and the Propylaea—in revising the words of the Panegyricus, more than a soldier fears the sound of arms, the clash of phalanxes, and wounds, fearing lest he should clash a vowel with a vowel, or utter a member, as they call it, shorter by one syllable.
