# sent_id = orat17.3.153
# text = Qui quum bello non delectetur quamvis strenuus, neque eo nisi ad tutandam utatur patriam, contingat illi grata pax, artibus et disciplinis florentissima, ut ille a prioribus requiescat aerumnis, et fruatur civium amore ac prosperitatibus, quarum auctor ipse est, fruatur domestica felicitate, qua illum imprimis beari scimus omnes, ac nuper, quum filia ex iuncti Occidentis et Orientis solio in natalem redux domum patri dilectissimo in aliquod redderetur tempus, non sine communi urbis laetitia vidimus.
# reading_translation = Since he, although vigorous, does not delight in war, nor uses it except for the protection of his country, may that pleasant peace, most flourishing in arts and disciplines, befall him, so that he may rest from his former hardships, and enjoy the love and prosperity of his citizens, of which he himself is the author, and enjoy domestic happiness, by which we all know he is especially blessed, and recently, when his daughter, returned from the throne of the united West and East to her natal home, was given back for some time to her most beloved father, we saw him not without the common joy of the city.
# literal_translation = Who since with war not delights although vigorous, nor it except for protecting uses country, may-befall him pleasing peace, with arts and disciplines most-flourishing, so that he from former may-rest hardships, and may-enjoy citizens' love and prosperities, of-which author himself is, may-enjoy domestic happiness, by-which him especially to-be-blessed we-know all, and recently, when daughter from joined West's and East's throne into natal returned home to-father most-beloved for some time was-given-back, not without common of-city joy we-saw.
