# sent_id = orat18.2.10
# text = tamen omnia debemus ad mentis notiones revocare, atque etiam in iis, quae sub sensus cadunt, divini humanique intellectus impressa rimari vestigia, maiora quidem pluris aestimantes, sed ne minora quidem, quae saepe maioribus inexspectatam lucem afferunt, eo usque pertaesi, ut nolimus etiam litteras suos habere rhyparographos et catatexitechnos et minutissimorum opusculorum fabricatores, quales Myrmecides et Callicrates sculptores olim fuerunt, non sine auctore natura, quae et ipsa sibi in subtilissimis rebus quibusque diligentissime struendis placet.
# reading_translation = Nevertheless, we must refer everything to the notions of the mind, and even in those things that fall under the senses, we must search out the traces of divine and human intellect impressed upon them, valuing the greater ones more highly, but not being so disgusted even with the lesser ones, which often bring unexpected light to the greater, to the point that we are unwilling for letters to have their own painters of filth and artisans of detail and fabricators of the tiniest works, such as Myrmecides and Callicrates, sculptors, once were, not without nature as their author, which itself takes pleasure in constructing even the most subtle things with the greatest care.
# literal_translation = Nevertheless all things we ought to the notions of the mind to refer, and also in those, which under the senses fall, of divine and human intellect impressed to search out traces, the greater indeed more highly valuing, but not the lesser even, which often to the greater unexpected light bring, to such an extent disgusted, that we may not wish even letters to have their own rhyparographers and catatexitechnicians and of the tiniest works fabricators, such as Myrmecides and Callicrates sculptors once were, not without author nature, which itself to itself in the most subtle things each most diligently constructing pleases.
# generator = deepseek-chat
