# sent_id = orat18.2.25
# text = Perfectus quippe in quoque genere artifex, poetica pulchrum gignendi praeditus virtute, quam omnibus artibus communem esse et Plato praecepit, et Simonides Ceus pridem significarat, ubi picturam esse poesim silentem, et poesim picturam loquentem pronuntiavit, innatam cuique rei vitalem immortalemque speciem, ideam vocant philosophi, illa quam dixi imaginatione, vix ullo alio adiumento, comprehensam repraesentat opere, in quo anima mirabili modo cum iis, quae sensibus percipias, conflata est:
# reading_translation = Indeed, the perfect artist in every genre, endowed with the poetic power of generating beauty, which both Plato taught to be common to all arts, and Simonides of Ceos had long before indicated, when he declared that painting is silent poetry and poetry is speaking painting, represents in his work the innate vital and immortal form of each thing, which philosophers call the idea, comprehended by that imagination I mentioned, with scarcely any other aid, in which the soul is wonderfully fused with those things that you perceive through the senses.
# literal_translation = Perfect indeed in each genre artist, with poetic of-beauty generating endowed power, which to all arts common to-be both Plato taught, and Simonides Ceus long-before had-signified, when painting to-be poetry silent, and poetry painting speaking he-pronounced, innate to-each thing vital and-immortal form, idea call philosophers, that which I-said imagination, scarcely any other aid, comprehended represents in-work, in-which soul wonderfully with those, which by-senses you-perceive, is-fused:
