# sent_id = orat18.2.121
# text = saltem concedetis, auditores, sive tragoediis seu pictura vel sculptura, quam posse ad tragoediam accedere proxime, Nioba et liberi monstrant, misericordiam movent ac metum, artifices perfectos simul efficere, ne miseratione et horrore spectantium opprimantur animi, sed ut, dum metuis, dum miseraris, idem sentias, nostrae huius misericordiae et timoris mollitie superiores esse illos, qui mala perpessi sunt, heroes, altaque mente et invicta tolerantes evasisse fluxae huius vitae casus, sicuti divinus generis humani servator, in quo fingendo iure suo multum elaborarunt artifices, mortis superavit dolores, nec cum cupiditatibus et per ipsas interisse nisi id, quod illi ex mortali traxerant contagio, divina aura eo reduce, unde erat profecta.
# reading_translation = You will at least concede, listeners, whether in tragedies or in painting or sculpture, which can approach tragedy most closely, as Niobe and her children show, that they arouse pity and fear, and at the same time produce perfect artists, so that the minds of the spectators are not overwhelmed by compassion and horror, but that, while you fear, while you pity, you feel the same thing, that those heroes who have endured misfortunes are superior to this softness of our pity and fear, and that by enduring with lofty and unconquered mind they have escaped the chances of this fleeting life, just as the divine savior of the human race, in portraying whom artists have rightly labored much, overcame the pains of death, and that nothing perished with his desires and through them except that which they had drawn from mortal contagion, the divine breath returning to the place from which it had set out.
# literal_translation = at least you will concede, listeners, whether in tragedies or in painting or sculpture, which to be able to approach tragedy most closely, Niobe and children show, pity they move and fear, artists perfect at the same time they bring about, lest by compassion and horror of the spectators be overwhelmed the minds, but that, while you fear, while you pity, the same you feel, to this our pity and fear softness superior to be those, who evils have endured, heroes, and with lofty mind and unconquered enduring to have escaped of this fleeting life the chances, just as the divine of the human race savior, in whom portraying by right much have labored artists, of death overcame the pains, nor with desires and through them to have perished except that, which they from mortal had drawn contagion, divine breath to that place returning, from where it had set out.
# generator = deepseek-v4-pro
