# reading_translation = Piety, old-fashioned frugality at home, public munificence, and finally the long-continued practice of workshops enriched Greece above all with so many ornaments; how great this was, you may infer from the fact that Lysippus alone is said to have made fifteen hundred works, and that three hundred and sixty statues, of which many were equestrian or stood on chariots or bigae, were set up by the Athenians for Demetrius of Phalerum, completed in less than three hundred days.
