# sent_id = orat19.3.20
# text = Profecto is, qui Platonis sententiam, felicem fore civitatem, si aut philosophi imperent, aut imperantes philosophentur, non modo in ore habuit, sed suo exemplo demonstrare conatus est, et qui quamvis sapiens non quod sibi, sed quod prudentissimis placeret, ratum esse voluit, profitens aequius esse, ut ipse tot et talium amicorum consilia sequeretur, quam ut tot et tales amici unius ipsius sequerentur voluntatem, is inquam non omisisse quidquam censendus est, quo civium adiuvaretur felicitas.
# reading_translation = Indeed, he who not only had on his lips Plato's opinion that a state would be happy if either philosophers ruled or rulers philosophized, but also tried to demonstrate it by his own example, and who, although wise, did not want what pleased himself but what pleased the wisest to be ratified, declaring that it was more just that he himself should follow the counsels of so many and such friends than that so many and such friends should follow the will of one man alone—he, I say, is not to be thought to have omitted anything by which the happiness of the citizens might be furthered.
# literal_translation = Indeed, he who of Plato the opinion, happy to be a state, if either philosophers rule, or rulers philosophize, not only in mouth had, but by his own example to demonstrate tried, and who although wise not what to himself, but what to the wisest pleased, ratified to be wanted, declaring more just to be, that he himself so many and of such friends counsels should follow, than that so many and such friends of one himself should follow will, he I say not to have omitted anything is to be thought, by which of citizens might be furthered happiness.
# generator = deepseek-chat
