# sent_id = orat19.3.49
# text = Antoninum, ita nos Fridericum Guilelmum divinitus missum rei publicae esse quum pridem praedicavimus, tum praedicamus quum maxime, summoque numini habemus gratiam, quod, si nostra aetas et naturae mala insolita ferre, et videre eas res debuit, in quibus rebus immota stare civitas vix posset, nisi is regnaret princeps, qui caritatis mutuae vinculo obstringeret suorum animos, rex nobis obtigit iustissimus, clementissimus, benignissimus, in cuius amore acquiesceret res publica.
# reading_translation = Antoninus, so we have long proclaimed that Frederick William was divinely sent to the state, and we proclaim it now especially, and we give thanks to the supreme deity, because, if our age had to endure both the unusual evils of nature and to see those things in which the state could scarcely stand firm, unless that prince reigned who would bind the minds of his people with the bond of mutual love, a king has fallen to our lot who is most just, most merciful, most kind, in whose love the state might find rest.
# literal_translation = Antoninus, so we Frederick William divinely sent to the state to be, when long ago we proclaimed, then we proclaim now especially, and to the supreme deity we have gratitude, that, if our age both the evils of nature unusual to bear, and to see those things ought, in which things unmoved to stand the state could scarcely, unless he reigned prince, who of mutual love with the bond would bind the minds of his people, a king to us fell to lot most just, most merciful, most kind, in whose love might rest the state.
