# text = ac si Aristoteles artibus minus iniquus fuit, quarum naturam ut reliquarum rerum omnium indagare conatus est, eas certe posthac philosophia si non disertis verbis tamen neglectione sua longum per tempus contempsit, donec quas aspernata erat florentes, superiori saeculo amplexa est senescentes et magna ex parte iacentes, novoque vocabulo quamvis incommodo novam formavit disciplinae suae partem, quae tota in artium versaretur principiis constituendis.
# reading_translation = And if Aristotle was not too hostile to the arts, whose nature he tried to investigate like that of all other things, philosophy certainly afterwards, if not in express words, yet by its neglect, for a long time despised them, until, when they were flourishing, it embraced them in the previous century when they were aging and for the most part lying prostrate, and with a new name, although inconvenient, formed a new part of its discipline, which would be entirely concerned with establishing the principles of the arts.
# literal_translation = And if Aristotle to arts less unfavorable was, whose nature as of all other things to investigate he tried, them certainly afterwards philosophy if not with express words nevertheless with its neglect for a long time despised, until which it had spurned flourishing, in the previous century it embraced aging and for the most part lying, and with a new word although inconvenient a new formed part of its discipline, which entirely in the principles of arts to be established would be occupied.
