# sent_id = orat22.1.1
# text = Fuit anni millesimi octingentesimi noni mensis Augusti dies sextus decimus, minister Regis illustrissime, auditores omnium ordinum spectatissimi, quum Fridericus Guilelmus Regimontii, ubi tum sedem regni habebat, rescriberet ad plures ex ministris et consiliariis, placere sibi et rata esse, quae illi de condendo Berolini studio generali, ut vocabant maiores, rettulissent, atque eam sibi rem adeo videri gravem tantoque momento futuram ad altiorem animorum eruditionem non solum in re publica Borussica verum etiam ultra huius fines latius disseminandam et propagandam, ad viros in quovis genere litterarum principes alliciendos et huic imperio conciliandos, postremo ad academiarum Berolinensium variorumque, quos haec urbs contineret, apparatuum et institutorum litteris inservientium quasi in unum corpus coniunctionem cum fructu perficiendam, ut nollet differre institutionem talis scholae, quae antiquo universitatis litterariae nuncupata nomine et iure honorum academicorum impertiendi instructa et ornata in hac urbe et in his, ubi nunc convenimus, collocaretur aedibus.
# reading_translation = It was the sixteenth day of August in the year 1809, most illustrious minister of the King, most respected listeners of all ranks, when Frederick William, at Königsberg, where he then had the seat of his kingdom, wrote back to several of his ministers and councilors that what they had reported to him about founding a general institution of learning at Berlin, as our ancestors called it, was pleasing to him and ratified, and that this matter seemed to him so weighty and destined to be of such great moment for the higher cultivation of minds, not only in the Prussian state but also to be disseminated and propagated more widely beyond its borders, for attracting men preeminent in every kind of learning and winning them over to this realm, and finally for bringing about, with profit, the union, as it were into one body, of the Berlin academies and the various apparatuses and institutions serving learning which this city contained, that he was unwilling to postpone the establishment of such a school, which, called by the ancient name of a literary university and furnished and adorned with the right of conferring academic honors, should be placed in this city and in these buildings where we now are assembled.
# literal_translation = It was of the year one-thousand eight-hundred ninth of the month of August the day six tenth, minister of the King most illustrious, listeners of all orders most respected, when Frederick William at Königsberg, where then the seat of the kingdom he was having, wrote back to several from ministers and councilors, to be pleasing to himself and ratified to be, what those about founding at Berlin a study general, as they were calling ancestors, had reported, and that to himself the matter to seem so weighty and of so great moment to be about to be for higher of minds cultivation not only in the state Prussian but also beyond of this the borders more widely to be disseminated and to be propagated, for men in any kind of letters preeminent to be attracted and to this empire to be won over, finally for of the academies Berlin and of the various, which this city was containing, apparatuses and institutions to letters serving as if into one body union with profit to be accomplished, that he was unwilling to postpone the institution of such a school, which by the ancient of university literary called name and with the right of honors academic of conferring instructed and adorned in this city and in these, where now we are assembled, should be placed buildings.
