# sent_id = orat24.2.73
# text = Quis neget hoc in bonis huius aevi potissimis censendum esse, quod desiimus in delictis vindicandis gaudere saevitia, et intelligimus plerique omnes, acerbissimas, quibus maiores nostri usi sunt frequentissime, poenas non convenire nisi rudibus saeculis moribusque et ingeniis incultis quae sibi persuaserint immanitatem sceleris vel aequandam vel superandam esse immanitate supplicii, ut truces ferocesque homines deterrerentur crudelitate vindictae?
# reading_translation = Who would deny that this must be counted among the most important blessings of this age, that we have ceased to take pleasure in cruelty when punishing offenses, and that most of us understand that the most severe punishments, which our ancestors employed very frequently, are not suited except to rude ages and to uncultivated morals and minds, which have persuaded themselves that the savagery of a crime must be either equaled or surpassed by the savagery of the punishment, so that fierce and savage men might be deterred by the cruelty of retribution?
# literal_translation = Who would deny this among the goods of this age most important to be counted, that we have ceased in offenses punishing to rejoice in cruelty, and we understand most all, that the most bitter, which our ancestors used very frequently, punishments not to be suitable except to rude ages and to morals and minds uncultivated which have persuaded to themselves that the savagery of crime either to be equaled or to be surpassed by the savagery of punishment, so that fierce and savage men might be deterred by the cruelty of vengeance?
