# reading_translation = Therefore, since I have often deliberately discussed what Frederick William had done for literature and the arts throughout so many years of his reign, although they were troubled by the tumults of wars, with what care and at what great expense he had provided for every kind of school, not only, as ambitious princes are wont to do, for those from whose dignity some splendor might seem to accrue to the empire, but especially with what zeal, with what liberality, with what equitable laws he had founded, adorned, and cherished this our seat of learning, I have often discoursed at length.
