# sent_id = orat25.4.62
# text = prorsus ut saltatrici vel cantrici mimaeve invideri lautiora sua salaria saepe audivimus, ne hoc quidem recte, quod etiam delicatis artibus, quae movent et delectant cum oculis et auribus animos elegantiorum sensumque pulchri formant, aliquid tribuendum est apud cultiores populos, et quod, quae exsequi nemo alius potest, quia vocis et gestuum gratiae naturae bono obtingunt, si tamen ea audire et spectare operae pretium est, tanto sunt largius remuneranda, quanto plures reppereris viliore mercede, qui vulgaribus negotiis obeundis pares sint.
# reading_translation = Just as we have often heard that a dancer or a singer or a mime actress is envied for her rather generous salaries, not even this is right, because even to the refined arts, which move and delight the minds of the more elegant with eyes and ears and form a sense of beauty, something is owed among more cultivated peoples, and because those things which no one else can perform, since the graces of voice and gesture fall to one by a gift of nature, if indeed it is worth the effort to hear and see them, are to be remunerated so much more generously, the more people you will have found who are equal to performing common tasks for a cheaper wage.
# literal_translation = Entirely as to-a-dancer or to-a-singer or to-a-mime-actress to-be-envied her-rather-generous salaries often we-have-heard, not even this rightly, because even to-refined arts, which move and delight with eyes and ears the-minds of-the-more-elegant and a-sense of-beauty form, something is-to-be-granted among more-cultivated peoples, and because, which to-perform no one else can, because of-voice and of-gesture graces by-a-gift of-nature befall, if nevertheless those to-hear and to-see of-effort worth is, by-so-much are more-generously to-be-remunerated, by-how-much more you-will-have-found with-a-cheaper wage, who for-common tasks to-be-performed equal are.
