# sent_id = orat25.4.86
# text = Rarum sane huius rei exemplum tantoque illustrius est Imperator Hadrianus, ambiguae princeps famae, sed ingenii facultate immensa, et qui virtutibus et benefaciendo superasse vitia naturae haud exigua videtur, nec saepe oblitus esse quod pollicitus erat, ita se rem publicam gesturum, ut sciret populi rem esse, non suam:
# reading_translation = A rare example of this matter, and so much the more illustrious, is the Emperor Hadrian, a prince of ambiguous reputation, but of immense intellectual ability, and who seems to have surpassed the not inconsiderable vices of his nature by his virtues and by doing good, and not often to have forgotten what he had promised, that he would so conduct the state as to know that it was the people's affair, not his own:
# literal_translation = Rare indeed of this thing an example and so-much-the-more illustrious is Emperor Hadrian, of-ambiguous reputation a prince, but of-ability immense, and who by-virtues and by-doing-good to-have-surpassed vices of-nature not small seems, and not often to-have-forgotten that-which he-had-promised, thus himself the state to-be-about-to-conduct, as that-he-might-know of-people the-affair to-be, not his-own:
