# sent_id = orat26.4.5
# text = tum qui eruditionem per regnum suum iam disseminatam acceperit, ut accepit Fridericus Guilelmus, quod nova quaeque aetas nova sibi adminicula deposcit, nec quae prioris aetatis necessitatibus sufficere videbantur, sufficiunt praesenti, immensam habet litteris benefaciendi materiam, quae in eo posita est, ut suppleat quae maiores imperfecta reliquerunt, ut si praesertim prolati fines imperii fuerint, novas bonarum artium addat sedes, ut scholas quaslibet et cetera litterarum instituta ad eum, qui adductus sit, meliorem eruditionis et scientiae statum et ad communem utilitatem accommodet, denique ut nihil omittat, quo et conservari parta et ad bonam frugem praesentia converti, et nova artium incrementa parari, nova lumina elici latiusque spargi possint.
# reading_translation = Then he who has received a culture already spread throughout his kingdom, as Frederick William received it, because each new age demands new aids for itself, and the things that seemed sufficient for the needs of the previous age do not suffice for the present, has an immense field for benefiting literature, which consists in this: that he supply what his ancestors left unfinished; that, especially if the boundaries of the empire have been extended, he add new seats of the liberal arts; that he adapt all schools and other institutions of learning to that better state of culture and knowledge which has been attained, and to the common good; finally, that he omit nothing by which both what has been acquired may be preserved, and present things may be turned to good account, and new advances of the arts may be prepared, and new lights may be drawn forth and spread more widely.
# literal_translation = Then he who culture through kingdom his already spread has received, as received Frederick William, because new each age new for itself aids demands, nor which of previous age for necessities to suffice seemed, suffice for present, immense has for letters of benefiting material, which in this placed is, that he supply which ancestors unfinished left, that if especially extended boundaries of empire have been, new of good arts he add seats, that schools any and other of letters institutions to that, which has been reached, better of culture and knowledge state and to common utility he adapt, finally that nothing he omit, by which both to be preserved things acquired and to good fruit present things to be turned, and new of arts advances to be prepared, new lights to be drawn forth more widely and to be spread may be able.
