# sent_id = orat26.5.16
# text = Non ab re fuerit nec vobis opinor molestum, auditores, si quemadmodum Graecorum lyrici tragicique poetae priscorum heroum personis ita usi sunt, ut eorum describendis moribus, rebus gestis, fortuna prospera vel adversa, clarorum qui tum florebant hominum virtutes exprimerent vel rerum praesentium significarent condicionem, non quae res, qui viri essent, ad quos haec referri vellent, pronuntiantes, quod modo poesis ratio non patiebatur modo alia causa dissuadebat, sed relicta audientibus interpretatione, quibus tamquam in speculum inspicientibus ultro offerretur vel viri descripti vel rei significatae imago:
# reading_translation = It would not be beside the point, nor, I think, unpleasant to you, my hearers, if, just as the Greek lyric and tragic poets used the personae of ancient heroes in such a way that, by describing their characters, deeds, and fortune, whether prosperous or adverse, they expressed the virtues of the famous men who then flourished or signified the condition of present affairs—not declaring what thing or what men they meant, to whom these things were to be referred, because sometimes the nature of poetry did not allow it, sometimes another reason dissuaded them, but leaving the interpretation to the hearers, to whom, as if looking into a mirror, the image of the man described or the thing signified was offered of its own accord—
# literal_translation = Not from the matter it-would-be nor to-you I-think unpleasant, hearers, if just-as the Greek lyric and tragic poets of-ancient heroes with-personae so they-used, that by-their describing characters, deeds, fortune prosperous or adverse, of-famous who then flourished men virtues they-expressed or of-present-affairs they-signified condition, not what thing, what men they-were, to-whom these to-be-referred they-wished, declaring, because at-one-time poetry's nature not it-permitted at-one-time another cause it-dissuaded, but having-been-left to-the-hearers interpretation, to-whom as-if into a-mirror looking-inspecting of-itself was-offered either of-man described or of-thing signified image:
