# sent_id = orat27.3.30
# text = Quam egregio autem temperamento cum parsimonia sociavit liberalitatem et munificentiam, nec tenax opum nec profusus, multis sumptibus in litterarum artiumque florem et in ecclesiae necessitates erogatis, multis impensis ut succurreret provinciis vel fame vel pestilentia vel annonae vilitate vel fluminum exundatione afflictis, haud exiguas insuper pecunias ex privado fisco largitus, ut singulorum, qui ad ipsum confugissent, levaret inopiam et angustiam rerum!
# reading_translation = With what excellent moderation, moreover, did he combine liberality and munificence with frugality, being neither tenacious of wealth nor prodigal, having spent much on the flourishing of literature and the arts and on the necessities of the church, having spent much to aid provinces afflicted by famine, pestilence, low grain prices, or floods, and having also bestowed no small sums from his private treasury to relieve the poverty and straitened circumstances of individuals who had fled to him!
# literal_translation = With what excellent moderation moreover with frugality he joined liberality and munificence, and neither tenacious of wealth nor prodigal, with many expenses for the flourishing of literature and arts and for the necessities of the church having been paid out, with many expenses having been paid out so that he might aid provinces afflicted by famine or pestilence or by cheapness of grain or by overflowing of rivers, moreover not small sums of money from private treasury having been lavished, so that he might relieve the poverty and straitness of affairs of individuals who had fled to him!
