# text = Quum enim ante hos decem annos tot novae res, discordiae, seditiones, civilia arma finitimas infestarent nationes, pacata et immota stetit civitas Borussica, non tam quod arte et armis contineretur, quam quod Friderici Guilelmi beneficiis obstricti et obligati animi erant, et quamvis essent quae desiderarentur, mutua imperantis et parentium caritas compensabat ea pacta et iura, quae tum extorquere populi cupiebant.
# reading_translation = For when in the last ten years so many new things, discords, seditions, and civil wars were harassing the neighboring nations, the Prussian state stood peaceful and unmoved, not so much because it was held together by skill and arms, as because the minds of the citizens were bound and obliged by the benefits of Frederick William; and although there were things that were desired, the mutual affection of the ruler and the subjects compensated for those agreements and rights which the people then longed to extort.
