# text = Attamen viribus me quamvis defectum erigit et confirmat haec cogitatio, quod quemadmodum in Patris patriae beatissimi nataliciis de magnitudine muneris mei id remitti et concedi solebat, ut a Regis laudibus paullulum digrederer ad alia, quae cum rebus nostris coniuncta essent, sic etiam hodie suffecisse aliquatenus videbor vel certe veniam impetrabo, si tantum de Rege dilectissimo dixero, ut nemo pietatem nostram desideret, nemo non quantopere Domino clementissimo obstricti et devoti simus, intelligat, sed simul id mihi sumpsero, ut dum de regia virtute expono, haud verear aliquid de litterarum ratione admiscere, de quibus coram vobis, qui in his occupati estis, et in sollemnibus Principi litterarum amantissimo dicatis disputantem quis est paullo cultior qui arbitraturus sit in re versari ab instituto aliena aut longe repetita?
# reading_translation = Nevertheless, though I am weak in strength, this thought raises and strengthens me: that just as on the birthday of our most blessed Father of the Fatherland, it was customary to allow and grant me, regarding the greatness of my task, to digress a little from the praises of the King to other matters which were connected with our affairs, so also today I shall seem to have done enough to some extent, or at least I shall obtain pardon, if I speak so much about our most beloved King that no one misses our devotion, and everyone understands how greatly we are bound and devoted to our most merciful Lord; but at the same time I shall take it upon myself, while I set forth the royal virtue, not to fear to mix in something about the study of literature, concerning which, when I am speaking before you, who are engaged in these pursuits, and on a day dedicated to the Prince who is most fond of literature, who is there of any refinement who would think that I am dealing with a matter foreign to the occasion or far-fetched?
# literal_translation = Nevertheless me though weak in strength raises and strengthens this thought, that just as on the birthday of the Father of the fatherland most blessed about the greatness of my task it to be remitted and granted was accustomed, that from the praises of the King a little I should digress to other things, which with our affairs connected were, so also today to have sufficed to some extent I shall seem or at least pardon I shall obtain, if so much about the King most beloved I shall have said, that no one our devotion misses, no one not how much to the Lord most merciful bound and devoted we are, understands, but at the same time this I shall have taken upon myself, that while about the royal virtue I set forth, not I should fear something about the study of literature to mix in, about which before you, who in these are occupied, and on solemn days to the Prince most fond of literature dedicated when one is speaking who is a little more cultured who would think that in a matter he is engaged foreign to the occasion or far-fetched?
