# text = Quanto igitur humanius Cicero, rerum experientia multo magis quam scholarum subtilitate formatus et in maximae omnium civitatis pulvere et palaestra exercitatus, philosophiam illam generi humano innatam, quam perficere sapientiae studiosi conantur, vitae ducem vocat, virtutis indagatricem expultricemque vitiorum, eamque dicit urbes peperisse et inventricem legum, magistram morum et disciplinae fuisse.
# reading_translation = How much more humane, therefore, is Cicero, formed by experience of affairs much more than by the subtlety of the schools, and exercised in the dust and wrestling-ground of the greatest of all states, who calls that philosophy innate to the human race, which those devoted to wisdom strive to perfect, the guide of life, the seeker-out and expeller of vices, and says that it gave birth to cities and was the inventor of laws, the teacher of morals and of discipline.
