# sent_id = orat28.3.84
# text = tantum meminerint velim, qui aliis rebus et technicis potissimum teneras puerorum et adolescentulorum mentes nutriri in scholis cupiunt, non id solum agi, ut ex Graecis Latinisque litteris futuri theologi et iurisconsulti tantum delibent, quantum ad disciplinae suae fontes mediocriter cognoscendos maxime necessarium sit, neque ut medici futuri artis suae vocabula discant, sed ut omnes, qui ad scientiam quamlibet accessuri sunt, opulentissima, quam priores gentes cultissimae succedentibus reliquerunt saeculis, perfruantur hereditate, atque ut optimis formentur exemplaribus, quibus plerique omnes, qui litteris inclaruerunt, formati sunt, quibus ipse Fridericus Magnus, qui tamen Gallicis potissimum litteris instructus erat, gymnasiorum alumnos institui voluit, non ille ratus aliis artibus aut noviciarum linguarum et librorum usu compensari illa studia posse;
# reading_translation = I would have them remember this above all, those who desire that the tender minds of boys and young men be nourished in schools chiefly by other subjects and technical ones: that it is not only a matter of future theologians and jurists sipping just so much from Greek and Latin literature as is most necessary for a moderate acquaintance with the sources of their discipline; nor that future physicians should learn the vocabulary of their art; but that all who are about to approach any branch of learning should enjoy to the full that most abundant inheritance which the most cultivated earlier generations left to succeeding ages, and that they should be moulded by the best models, by which almost all who have become famous in letters were formed, by which Frederick the Great himself, though he had been trained chiefly in French literature, wished the pupils of the gymnasia to be instructed, not thinking that those studies could be compensated by other arts or by the use of modern languages and books.
# literal_translation = only let-them-have-remembered I-would-wish, who by-other things and technical especially the-tender of-boys and of-young-men minds to-be-nourished in schools desire, not that alone to-be-done, that from Greek and Latin letters future theologians and jurists only may-sip, as-much-as to of-discipline their sources moderately to-be-known most necessary is, nor that physicians future of-art their words may-learn, but that all, who to knowledge any are-about-to-approach, the-most-opulent, which earlier generations most-cultivated to-succeeding left ages, may-enjoy inheritance, and that by-the-best they-may-be-formed models, by-which most all, who in-letters have-become-famous, formed are, by-which himself Frederick Great, who nevertheless in-French especially letters instructed had-been, of-gymnasia the-pupils to-be-instructed wished, not he having-thought by-other arts or of-modern languages and of-books use to-be-compensated those studies to-be-able;
