# sent_id = orat29.1.3
# text = eximit hos scrupulos ea cogitatio, quod nemo fere vel exspectat vel postulat, hodie ut nihil praeter Regis laudes disseramus, sed oratori, dummodo et suae et vestrae pietati satisfacere conetur, remittitur aliquid de oneris magnitudine, et permittitur tractare singularem aliquam materiam, talem certe, quae non ita aliena ab huius diei sanctitate et celebritate sit, ea ut neque ad Principis personam, ad Principis amorem et laudationem referri possit, neque animis vestris, qui ad Regem venerandum conveneritis, accommodata aliquatenus haberi.
# reading_translation = This thought removes these scruples: that almost no one expects or demands that today we should discuss nothing but the King's praises, but rather the orator, provided he tries to satisfy both his own and your piety, is granted some remission of the burden's magnitude, and is permitted to treat some particular subject, certainly such that it is not so alien to the sanctity and celebration of this day, so that it can neither be referred to the Prince's person, to the Prince's love and praise, nor be considered in any way suited to your minds, who have gathered to revere the King.
# literal_translation = Removes these scruples that thought, because no one almost either expects or demands, today that nothing except the King's praises we-discuss, but to-the-orator, provided-that both to-his-own and to-your piety he-tries to-satisfy, is-remitted something of the burden's magnitude, and is-permitted to-treat some particular subject, such certainly, which not so alien from this day's sanctity and celebration is, so-that it neither to the Prince's person, to the Prince's love and praise to-be-referred can, nor to-your minds, who to the King to-revere have-gathered, suited in-some-way to-be-considered.
