# sent_id = orat29.2.27
# text = Sed si patriae fines, ut dixi, ex populi finibus comparandi sunt, ac non tam solo quam societate hominum et civitate constat patria, concedendum est multos duplicem habere patriam, alteram artiorem, alteram latiorem, quia unus ille populus in plures gentes, et quod maius esse videtur, in plures civitates divisus est, sive eas prorsus disiunctas seu foedere coniunctas, atque aut rei publicae forma diversas aut, quod longe praestat, iisdem instructas institutis et legibus:
# reading_translation = But if the boundaries of the fatherland, as I said, are to be compared with the boundaries of the people, and the fatherland consists not so much in the soil as in the fellowship of men and in the state, it must be conceded that many have a double fatherland, one narrower, the other wider, because that one people is divided into several nations, and, what seems to be greater, into several states, whether they are completely separate or joined by treaty, and either different in form of government or, what is far better, equipped with the same institutions and laws.
# literal_translation = But if the boundaries of the fatherland, as I said, are to be compared from the boundaries of the people, and not so much by soil as by the fellowship of men and by the state does the fatherland consist, it must be conceded that many have a double fatherland, one narrower, the other wider, because that one people into several nations, and what greater to be seems, into several states divided is, whether them completely separate or by treaty joined, and either by the form of the state different or, what far excels, with the same institutions and laws equipped:
