# sent_id = orat29.2.28
# text = non enim unquam bene coalescent civitates, quae utantur legibus valde disparibus, sed suspectae alterae alteris erunt, tum quod eius, quae liberius constituta sit, cives diffident iis, quibus minus libera paret, et ab his libertati suae periculum imminere haud immerito iudicabunt, tum quod minus liberae huius rei publicae domini metuent, ne ex illa liberiore amor libertatis disseminetur ac propagetur in ipsorum subiectos atque hos reddat impatientiores imperii.
# reading_translation = For states that use very different laws will never coalesce well, but they will be suspicious of one another, both because the citizens of the one that has been established more freely will distrust those to which the less free one is subject, and will judge, not without reason, that danger to their liberty threatens from them, and because the masters of this less free republic will fear that from that more free one the love of liberty may be spread and propagated among their own subjects and may make them more impatient of rule.
