# sent_id = orat29.2.101
# text = Profecto aliqua acerbitas, quamvis molesta iis, qui libertati dicendi nondum assueverint, praestat ignavae patientiae, qua qui contentus sit, is non multum differt ab eo, quem Solon ille legum lator sapientissimus acri dignum animadversione iudicavit, eum dico, qui in discordia et seditione populi noluerit alteriusutrius partis esse, sed solitarius ac separatus a communi malo civitatis secesserit.
# reading_translation = Indeed, some bitterness, although it is troublesome to those who have not yet become accustomed to freedom of speech, is preferable to indolent patience; and he who is content with that patience differs not much from the man whom that Solon, the wisest lawgiver, judged worthy of severe punishment—I mean the man who, in the discord and sedition of the people, was unwilling to take the side of either party, but withdrew alone and apart from the common evil of the state.
# literal_translation = Indeed some bitterness, although troublesome to those who to freedom of speech not yet have become accustomed, is preferable to indolent patience, with which he who content may be, he not much differs from him, whom that Solon the lawgiver wisest worthy of severe punishment judged, him I mean, who in discord and sedition of the people was unwilling of either party to be, but solitary and separated from the common evil of the state withdrew.
