# sent_id = orat30.1.10
# text = Dicam igitur hodie de litteris, nec tamen de litteris generatim, sed quatenus de iis bene mereri principes possunt, ac ne hanc quidem rem tractabo integram, sed praemissa una et altera sententia magis generali continebo me posthac in ea quaestione, quid principis de singulis litterarum partibus iudicia conferre ad doctrinarum florem vel queant vel nequeant, eamque magno Friderici Magni exemplo illustrabo, quo uti tanto mihi videtur aptius esse, quanto Fridericus Guilelmus Quartus, quod ex veteri praecepto simile non cognoscitur nisi a simili, illius herois magis agnoscit ingenium, idque etiam ab aequalibus suis posterisque vult agnosci, quippe qui digna et illius et suo nomine monimenta a patre beatissimo vix inchoata condere praeclaro consilio summisque studiis instituerit.
# reading_translation = I will speak today, therefore, about literature, yet not about literature in general, but insofar as princes can deserve well of it; and I will not even treat this subject fully, but after first setting forth one or two more general observations, I will confine myself thereafter to the question of what the judgments of a prince concerning the individual branches of learning can or cannot contribute to the flourishing of scholarship, and I will illustrate this by the great example of Frederick the Great, which seems to me to be all the more appropriate to use, inasmuch as Frederick William the Fourth—since, according to the ancient precept, like is not known except by like—recognizes more fully the genius of that hero, and desires it to be recognized also by his contemporaries and posterity, inasmuch as he has undertaken, with illustrious purpose and the utmost zeal, to complete the monuments worthy of both his name and that of his most blessed father, which had scarcely been begun.
# literal_translation = I-will-speak therefore today about literature, and-yet not about literature in-general, but as-far-as of them well to-deserve princes can, and not even this indeed matter I-will-treat whole, but having-been-sent-ahead one and a-second observation more general I-will-confine myself afterwards in that question, what of-a-prince about individual of-letters parts judgments to-bring-together to of-doctrines flower either they-can or they-cannot, and-it by-the-great of-Frederick the-Great example I-will-illustrate, which to-use by-so-much to-me seems more-suitable to-be, by-how-much Frederick William the-Fourth, because from old precept like not is-known except by like, of-that hero more recognizes genius, and-it also by contemporaries his and-posterity wishes to-be-recognized, inasmuch-as who worthy both of-him and his name monuments by father most-blessed scarcely begun to-found with-distinguished plan and-highest zeal has-undertaken.
