# sent_id = orat30.3.172
# text = Namque etsi non dubitavit sententiam suam privatus dicere de rebus, de quibus vel homines nulla neque ingenii vi nec copia doctrinae antecellentes iudicare et olim soliti sunt et quum maxime solent, tamen, ut imprimis regium est sibi diffidere in multis et audire eos, qui in quaque arte praestent, ea, quae ipsi videbantur de tradendis litteris, nequaquam exsecutus est.
# reading_translation = For although he did not hesitate, as a private citizen, to express his opinion on matters about which even men who excel in no force of talent nor abundance of learning have both been accustomed to judge from of old and are especially accustomed at the present time, nevertheless, as it is especially kingly to distrust oneself in many things and to listen to those who excel in each art, he by no means carried out what seemed to him best regarding the teaching of letters.
# literal_translation = For although he did not doubt his opinion as-a-private-citizen to say about things, about which even men neither by force of-talent nor by abundance of-learning excelling to judge and formerly have-been-accustomed and especially now are-accustomed, nevertheless, as especially kingly it-is to-distrust oneself in many-things and to-hear those, who in each art excel, those-things, which themselves seemed about handing-down letters, by-no-means he-carried-out.
