# sent_id = orat30.3.202
# text = Immo vero id ipsum, quod a me nunc expositum est, persuasum habere sapientissimum Regem, vir illustrissimus, cui ille institutionis publicae curam commisit, planissime et certissime declaravit, quum oratione apud Bonnenses nuperrime habita exponeret, nolle Regem ad novum et meliorem, quem adducturus sit, statum adducendum suo iussu et imperio uti, neque eum de hac re mittere mandata ad ministros suos nec decreta facere, sed cupere tantum atque exoptare, ut multa ingenia et vires excitentur, quae agant sua sponte.
# reading_translation = Indeed, the most illustrious man, to whom the king entrusted the care of public education, declared most clearly and certainly that the wisest king was persuaded of this very thing which has now been set forth by me, when in a speech recently delivered at Bonn he explained that the king did not wish to use his own command and authority to bring about a new and better state of affairs, which he is about to introduce, nor to send mandates to his ministers about this matter, nor to make decrees, but only to desire and long for many talents and energies to be roused, which should act of their own accord.
