# text = vir ingenio, virtute, auctoritate spectatissimus, qui Lipsiae eandem quam ego in hac urbe artem profitetur, nescio an illis verbis provocatus concessit quidem solis defectionibus, quas oculis cernimus, hodie neminem terreri, sed alias dixit easque perniciosissimas conduplicari tenebras et occaecare noctis ac nimbum nigrorem, qui eo tantum a deficiente sole differat, quod solis orbem quando relictura sit umbra sciamus, ex istis autem tenebris nec quando neque an lux ac non potius crassa caligo sit emersura, sit cuiquam cognitum.
# reading_translation = A man most distinguished in talent, virtue, and authority, who at Leipzig professes the same art that I do in this city, whether provoked by those words, I do not know, conceded indeed that no one today is frightened by the eclipses of the sun which we behold with our eyes, but he said that other darknesses, and those most pernicious, are doubled and that the blackness of night and clouds blinds us, which differs from the eclipsing sun only in this, that we know when the shadow is about to leave the sun's disk, but from those darknesses neither when nor whether light and not rather thick gloom will emerge is known to anyone.
# literal_translation = A man by talent, by virtue, by authority most distinguished, who at Leipzig the same as I in this city art professes, I know not whether by those words provoked conceded indeed to the eclipses of the sun, which with eyes we behold, today no one to be frightened, but other ones he said and those most pernicious to be doubled darknesses and to blind the blackness of night and of clouds, which only in this from the eclipsing sun differs, that the sun's disk when about to leave the shadow we know, but from those darknesses neither when nor whether light and not rather thick gloom is about to emerge, to anyone to be known.
