# sent_id = orat31.3.89
# text = Neque enim ille ea tantum beneficentiae parte, quae in externis bonis posita est, inferiores incolarum classes impertit, sed quoniam, quo amplior divitibus facultas est liberos omni genere eruditionis et elegantioris doctrinae instruendi, eo neglectior solet rusticae et in frequentibus potissimum urbibus operariae multitudinis educatio atque institutio esse, curabit, ut haec quoque eius discipline particeps fiat, sine qua hominum vita ne humana quidem esse videatur, neve litteris et artibus laetissime florentibus, ut floruerunt in Aegypto regnantibus Macedonicae stirpis regibus et in Italia Mediceis aliisque principibus munificentissimis, nihilo minus vulgus, quod iisdem temporibus factum videmus, misere ac serviliter derelinquatur incultum.
# reading_translation = For he does not bestow upon the lower classes of inhabitants only that part of beneficence which is placed in external goods, but since, the greater the opportunity the rich have of instructing their children in every kind of learning and of more elegant education, the more neglected is wont to be the education and training of the rustic and, especially in populous cities, of the working multitude, he will take care that this also may become a partaker of that discipline, without which human life does not even seem to be human, and that the common people, while letters and arts flourish most happily, as they flourished in Egypt under the kings of Macedonian stock and in Italy under the Medici and other most munificent princes, may not, as we see happened in the same times, be left miserably and servilely uncultivated.
# literal_translation = For neither he those only beneficence part, which in external goods placed is, lower inhabitants classes shares, but since, the more ample to the rich opportunity is children every kind of learning and of more elegant education instructing, the more neglected is wont rustic and in populous especially cities working multitude education and training to be, he will take care, that this also of that discipline partaker may become, without which of men life not even human to be may seem, nor letters and arts most happily flourishing, as they flourished in Egypt ruling Macedonian stock kings and in Italy Medici and other princes most munificent, nevertheless the common people, which in the same times done we see, miserably and servilely may be left uncultivated.
