# reading_translation = Indeed, Erasmus, in a book most full of good fruit, which he wrote on the education of a Christian prince to Charles, the grandson of Maximilian, who shortly afterward was created Emperor, did not hesitate to go so far as to affirm, having spurned the custom prudently and decorously received, as my opinion holds, in the titles of princes, that dominion, empire, kingdom, majesty, and power are words of the pagans, and to declare that the Christian empire is nothing other than an administration, a guardianship, a beneficence.
