# text = et quum Fridericus Guilelmus Tertius, Pater patriae beatissimus, quamquam instaurandam intellexerat rem publicam novoque modo novisque consultis emendandam atque exornandam, tamen qua erat continentia et temperantia, maxime id studeret, ut aequabili tranquillitate aevum traduceret civitas, atque ut hanc magis instrueret viribus, quam post tot turbas, tot rerum conversiones, tot toleratos labores moveri has novas vires vellet;
# reading_translation = And although Frederick William III, the most blessed Father of his country, had understood that the state needed to be restored and to be corrected and adorned in a new way and with new counsels, yet with his characteristic self-restraint and moderation, he especially strove that the commonwealth might pass its time in calm tranquility, and that he might rather strengthen it with forces than wish these new forces to be stirred up after so many disturbances, so many revolutions, so many endured labors;
# literal_translation = And when Frederick William Third, Father of fatherland most-blessed, although to-be-restored he-had-understood the republic and in-new-way and with-new-counsels to-be-corrected and to-be-adorned, nevertheless with-which he-was self-restraint and temperance, especially this he-would-strive, that in-even tranquility age might-pass the state, and that this rather he-might-strengthen with-forces, than after so-many disturbances, so-many of-things reversals, so-many endured labors to-be-moved these new forces he-would-wish;
