# sent_id = orat33.2.17
# text = Nimirum quum superioribus saeculis res politica ad sola fere externa, ad regnorum inter sese amicitias et inimicitias, ad foedera et pacem et bellum, ad finium propagationem atque acquirendas vel armis vel pactis vel nuptiis terras referretur, atque in scientia hac optimus videretur, qui esset ad fingendum et fallendum sollertissimus et callidissimus, non arte utens sed artibus, iusta vero politices notio, quam Graeci instituerant et praestantissimi quique recentiores auctores excoluerant, in plurimis civitatibus et maxime in Germania esset iamiam prope oblivioni tradita;
# reading_translation = Indeed, when in earlier centuries political affairs were referred almost solely to external matters, to friendships and enmities among kingdoms, to treaties and peace and war, to the extension of boundaries and to acquiring lands either by arms or by agreements or by marriages, and in this science he seemed the best who was most skillful and most cunning at inventing and deceiving, not using art but arts, while the just notion of politics, which the Greeks had established and the most excellent recent authors had cultivated, was in most states and especially in Germany already almost consigned to oblivion;
# literal_translation = Indeed when in earlier centuries the political matter to almost only external things, to friendships and enmities of kingdoms among themselves, to treaties and peace and war, to extension of boundaries and to acquiring lands either by arms or by agreements or by marriages was referred, and in this science the best seemed, who was for inventing and deceiving most skillful and most cunning, not using art but arts, the just notion of politics, which the Greeks had established and the most excellent each recent authors had cultivated, in most states and especially in Germany was already almost to oblivion consigned;
